If you have any more information on this, I'm sure that everybody
here would be greatly interested.  Questions of particular interest:
what does "national" mean?  What hardware will be required to hook
one's PC up to this net?  I would certainly pay $70-$100/mo for a
56kbps line into my home, but I fear that "national" will mean
parts of california and new york and boston.

> It is hard to get all the facts, but many articles report this a being
> a bi-directional 10 mbps connection. I have heard other reports
> suggesting it is 1.54 mbps into the home, and 56 kps or 256 kps out of
> the home...

Well, I'm a bit skeptical about > 1 mbps links via current cable
TV links -- It seems to me that it would be hard for the entire
metropilitan broadband network to do much better than 100 mbps
(though a gigabit might be feasible) which would mean that all
the users would have to share that bandwidth.

> I am interested in talking about 1) the effect this will have on
> the marketplace

The devil's in the details.  Particularly, the places where it's
available and how much the price drops.  I really truly hope that
this works out well because the market for personal internet tools
(like, coincidentally, I am developing!) will expand faster than
if centralized providers like aol, prodigy, compuserve, etc, provide
monolithic interfaces to their users.  A raw IP link is much more
flexible and will let front-end developers compete more effectively.

> 2) neat things we can do on this list with that
> much bandwidth and "free" CPU. (We can have our own IRC, or our
> own PGP encrypted "whatever.," we could have a mud or a moo, I will
> certainly start a exi-binaries@extropy.org mailing list... :)

Here's my suggestion: Start to phase things into WWW access.  I have
been studying WWW lately and it appears to be a very well-thought-out
system that is gaining in popularity quite rapidly.  Widespread
56kbps lines will make it simply explode.  Archives of all kinds,
as well as more "interactive" things like MUDs are all easy
to incorporate into WWW servers in such a way that any reasonably-written
client can interact with them quite well.

Maybe one way to think about what various capabilities will mean is
to consider some typical sizes for various objects and see what
the transfer time is like at various speeds:

This message:       5K
Small postscript:  30K
Static image:      50K
1 Minute audio:   300K
Large postscript: 800K
short video:     2000K
longer video:   20000K (we'll call that an upper limit for "messages" for now)

My 9600-baud modem:

(5 sec, 30 sec, 50 sec, 5 min, 13 min, 33 min, 5.5 hour)

If this is "current technology", this explains why small documents and
maybe a picture are about all that we'll send around right now.
Maybe it's useful to divide things into categories:
  Category 1: Default messages (we don't care much about filtering it): 10 sec
  Category 2: Low-frequency larks (don't care if it's a waste as long
	as it doesn't happen too often): 2 min
  Category 3: Rare, request only (something we want): 10 min
  The rest are too costly except to do very rarely

Currently, we're happy with our text messages and appreciate small
structured docs and images occasionally but would be unhappy to get
lots of them.

Now, a 56kbps line:

(1 sec, 4 sec, 7 sec, 43 sec, 2 min, 5 min, 47 min)

When this is widespread, it seems like sending images and structured
documents (standard multimedia mail) will be just fine as a matter
of routine, Audio messages, large documents, and short video clips
will also be OK occasionally, but not as a communication bread-and-butter.

1Mbps link (what we all hope for):

(40 ms, 250 ms, .5 sec, 3 sec, 7 sec, 20 sec, 3 min)

In this world, we get to the point where we will be able to send
illustrated, narrated multimedia mail as a matter of routine,
with video clips fairly common (what fun the WACO discussion
would have been with video!).

One interesting issue is what will happen as the disparity between
list members hooked in to these advanced networks and list members
not so hooked in...

derek


