From: Jeff Fabijanic <jeff@PROFESSOR-ED.ELF.COM>
Subject: form, space and order

Nancy writes:

>I've read about methods of learning to visualize 4-space in either
>Rucker or Gardner--I might be able to track them down if people care.

I'd be very interested in hearing more about 4-d learning techniques.

>I've met someone who said that she could visualize in 4-space, and
>I asked her some questions to check on it..

I also know a couple folks who seem to be able to do this at will (mostly fellow  
MITers - math majors - surprise surprise!). I have almost felt that I was able to  
do so on a few occasions, but it was a very tenuous and slippery experience at  
best.

>I asked her how many
>corners a tesseract had, and after several efforts during which
>she appeared to be visualizing, she said that she kept losing track
>of the corners when she tried to count them.

Hmm, now, I'm not able to visualize 4-space reliably, as I've said before. But I  
can visualize a tesseract's various shadows in three space, and it seems like  
that should be enough to give a reasonable answer. A tesseract has 16 vertices,  
32 edges, 20 faces, and contains 8 distinct 3-spaces. This just took me about 15  
seconds to do in my head, and I spent a couple minutes double checking with paper  
and pencil, and get the same answer. (Then again, maybe I'm wrong. Corrections by  
informed list members are welcome, of course).

>I asked her what a
>hypersphere looked like, and she said (fairly quickly) that it
>looked like an ordinary sphere, only rounder.

Love it! Whether she was legitimately visualizing or not, this is a Great Answer!

>Any theories about how you could tell whether someone else can
>visualize 4-space?
>
>Nancy Lebovitz

Tell them to visualize a tesseract. Now ask them the following questions:

1) Is there a straight 3-d line that they can scribe through each of the 3-d  
spaces contained in the tesseract in turn? [yes (an infinite number of them), but  
they don't run orthogonally to the faces or edges.]

2) If they answer correctly, ask them the fewest number of *orthogonal* 3-d lines  
necessary to pass through all the 3-spaces? [2]

3) How many such lines are needed to intersect each *face* of the tesseract? [3]

3) Assume that the tesseract's faces are solid (with 4 doors in them - one for  
each edge). If an ant were to start in the center of one face, crawl straight  
across to the center of the face of the 3-space in front of it, turn 90 degrees  
left, and crawl straight ahead to the center of the face *now* in front of it,  
turn left, continue ad nauseum, how soon would the ant end up back on the same  
face? [the ant would crawl across a total of 3 faces (going through 3 doors)  
before finding itself exactly where it started (in the same orientation as well)]

4) A square can be divided into four small squares with bisecting lines. A cube  
can be divided into eight small cubes with intersecting planes. How many cubes  
are necessary to divide a tesseract in a similar manner and how many tesseracts  
are produced? [4 and they produce 16 smaller tesseracts]

These are all very easy. in fact, someone who was simply familiar with the  
geometry of a tesseract but not necessarily able to visualize 4-d, could answer  
these quite readily. In fact, although I used 3-d shadow visualization to come up  
with these questions, now that I look at the answers I realize that some of the  
geometric progressions are very simplistic. So these could be "guessed" as well.  
I suppose the real test would be the speed with which one answered. I would  
expect that someone who could readily visualize 4-d would be able to answer these  
as quickly as someone who could 3-d visualize could answer the analogous  
questions of a cube.

 - Jeff




