Derek discusses bandwidth requirements and limitations.
He hits an important point: CATV is a shared-access
physical medium.  I regard T3 as about the limit for
a single CATV channel pair (1 inbound / 1 outbound)
using present technology.  This means you have 45 Mbps
or less to share among however many users you have on the
cable.  BUT this is NOT the entire metropolitan area:
modern systems use fiber to the curb, where a single
fiber serves 3000 or fewer households (heading towards
200 as the fiber gets closer and closer to the home).
So try dividing 45 Mbps (or whatever you derate my
estimate to) by whatever fraction of cable-connected
households you expect to have simultaneously active
on the MAN.

hi end estimate: 45 Mbps / 45 households -> 1 Mbps avg

lo end: 1.5 Mbps / 1500 households -> 1 kbps avg

I believe that economic incentives will drive the
fiber close enough to the home to make CATV just
enough cheaper than telco offerings so that it will
be a viable business for the CATV operators.  My lo
end estimate would not get people to switch, so
the operators won't go that low.

BTW -- the cable ops know that they must drive the
monthly charges way down from PSI's original estimate
in order to sign up many subscribers.
