Subject: TECH: What I would do with 10 mpbs IP connection

First, to derek, who asks:
>Wait a minute... how would that work?  What information would come
>in through the cable as opposed to the phone line?

Probably all of it.  A net connection, like a telephone, has a
receiver and a transmitter.  It doesn't matter if they both actually
use the same carrier or not - for instance, you could hook up an
earphone to your friend's microphone on one wire, and a microphone to
your friend's earphone via a radio link; it would still work like a
regular telephone.  The benefit of the incoming connection being the
high bandwidth one relates to the "lurker" discussion that's been
going on recently.  It's estimated that about 80% of net users are
primarily information consumers, as opposed to information producers.
If you're spending lots of time reading news, downloading files via
FTP, etc..., it makes sense to have a high speed connection into your
house, whereas if you *aren't* running an FTP site, exploding a
mailing list, or generating broadcast audio, you probably don't need a
connection like that for outgoing traffic.

This also dovetails well with the cable technology - right now, there
is no good way to get information *from* the endpoints (houses) back
to the hub.  It's a broadcast medium.  So systems like GTE's "Main
Street" and the future IP linkup use phone lines to communicate menu
choices, commands, or whatever input comes from the user.

Now this raises a question - does anyone know how the PSI project
plans to switch the IP packets into the cable network?  The way Main
Street handles it is with a dedicated cable channel.  You have a box
sitting on top of your cable box that contains a modem, some
packet-switching hardware, and a video frame-grabber.  When you
connect to the system, the modem connects to their computer, and the
box starts scanning all the video packets (which are just video stills
tagged with a particular box-ID) on the "main street" channel.  Any
time it sees one go by with it's number, it puts it up on your screen
and holds it there until the next one.  (it's actually interesting to
tune to the "packetized" channel when you're NOT connected - you see
*all* the frames being transmitted to anyone using Main Street in your
area... "boy, lotsa people playing blackjack tonight...")

I assume that each cable channel has a certain amount of bandwidth
(anyone have a clue as to how much?), which is a fraction of the total
available across the wire.  So if the IP system uses the same "unused
channel" trick, perhaps the performance could be improved later by
adding extra channels of IP?  (I actually agree with whomever said
they doubted that 10mbps would be feasible on such a system - once
lots of people start using it, I wonder if you'll even see a reliable
256kbps into your home)

Oh, one more thing - currently, there is no way to acheive close to
56kbps over a normal phone line.  I believe with the new V.FAST
modems, people are actually getting close to 26k, but that doesn't
seem to work everywhere.  So right now, you need to lease a special
line from the phone company (and use a special modem) to get that kind
of bandwidth.  I don't know what outgoing data rates the PSI project
will actually support, but they won't be that high.

		--Gub (Glen Daniels, ELF Communications)

